{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Perhaps my father will feel me</b> — The meaning is not that he would feel him in order to recognize him. Rather, he said: perhaps he will draw me close to himself to kiss me, or place his hand on my face, in the manner of a father's affection for his son, and he will discover through touching that I am smooth. And I am astonished how he was not afraid of recognition by the voice, for all people are recognized by their voices, as our Sages said (Gittin 23a): how is a blind man permitted to his wife, and how are people permitted to their wives at night? — through familiar recognition of the voice. And if ordinary people recognize thus, how much more so with Isaac the wise and the expert in recognizing his sons, so that he would truly have a fixed impression of the voice. Perhaps these brothers were similar in their voices, and therefore they said (Genesis Rabbah 65:19) that <b>\"the voice is the voice of Jacob\"</b> means his manner of speech — that he speaks in a gentle tone and mentions the Name of Heaven. Or perhaps he would change his voice to speak in the manner of his brother, for there are among people those who know how to do so."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The phrase בטביעות עינא דקלא is an Aramaic expression meaning literally 'the eye's familiarity with the voice'; I have rendered it as 'familiar recognition of the voice.' Ramban uses it in reference to the Talmudic discussion in Gittin 23a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:46.859230014Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}